\1
                       CE7 OVERVIEW

         This program is used to download channel and
    configuration information to the Vertex series of
    Yaesu radios.
         On line help is available by pressing F1 at
    any of the main menu entry fields, or on any of
    the submenus.

    CE7 Features

       . Mouse support.  Please ensure that your mouse
         driver is installed correctly.
       . The status bar along the bottom of the screen
         will also contain information such as function
         of the F keys, short help explanations, action
         keys or ranges of data entry.
       . Many of the hardware parameters are initialised
         to defaults when you select the type of radio.
         Make changes to these items only for custom
         applications.

                ** End of General Help **

\2
                    HARDWARE PARAMETERS

    Allows you to configure the radio for different
    frequency bands and for special applications. DO
    NOT CHANGE ANY OF THE FOLLOWING 4 ITEMS AS THEY ARE
    PRESET BY CHANGING THE FREQUENCY:

                       REF OSC XTAL
                    FIRST IF FREQUENCY
                       LO INJECTION
                         PRESCALER



\3
                      SCAN PARAMETERS

    Allows you to set up variables for scan operation
    and select various scan options, as well as what
    happens when you go off-hook, PTT, etc.


\4
                          VOTING

    Allows you to program the unit for Voting Search &
    Lock operation.  Program up to 10 groups of channels
    (VOTE GROUP 1 to 10) and allocate channels to the 
    groups.  Channels included in a vote group may be
    LOCKed if desired, to prevent the user accessing the
    channel directly.

\5
                      SEQUENTIAL TONE

    The various selections in sequential tone program-
    ming will greatly affect how the radio reacts to
    the user.  The tone systems works like this:

    Create a table of up to 16 tones (numbered 0-F) and
    a separate table of up to 8 tone durations (used
    for ENCODE ONLY).

    Next, program the types of decode sequences you
    would like to have active for this radio using the
    numbers from the tone table.  Tone sequences up to
    17 tones long may be programmed independent of the
    tone duration (no tone would act as a pause). There
    are SIX different possible decode sequences that
    the radio will act upon.  Some are dependent on the
    SQ flag set from the main menu, and some are radio
    wide (refer to the help function under DECODE
    ID's).

    There are up to 8 different ENCODE sequences that
    may be programmed the same way as the decode ID's:
    Select the tone number from the table, AND the dur-
    ation from the timing table.  As with the decode
    sequences, some encode ID's are channel dependent
    and some are radio wide (see help function under
    ENCODE ID's).



\6
                        FRONT PANEL

    Sets various operation parameters and selects
    different options available to the "A" button.
    See help function for each item for more details.


\7
                   MARKETING PARAMETERS

    Selects various dealer specific functions.  None of
    the features in this section are in use for the end
    user, and are only available at the manufacturer
    privilege level.



\8
                       MISCELLANEOUS

    Selects various dealer options and programming
    aids, as well as the timers for TOT, PENALTY, and
    RELEASE times.


\9
                          UPLOAD

    Used to read information from the radio and over
    write the existing data on screen.  Upload takes
    approximately 30 seconds.

\10
                         DOWNLOAD

    Used to write screen information to the radio.
    Data will be verified by program for errors before
    download (such as no TOT timer set if channels are
    enabled on main menu etc.)  Download takes
    approximately 30 seconds.

\11
                      CHANNEL NUMBER

    The Channel Number can take any value from 1..99
    Will be displayed as the 2 right most upper digits.
    Each group may have a channel 1, 2, etc.

\12
                      PRIORITY

    Each group can have up to 2 priorities; P2 will
    take precedence over a non-priority reception, and
    P1 will take precedence over P2; P1 cannot be
    interrupted.  Selecting any level of priority will
    automatically select priority scan during scanning.
    Use space bar or right mouse button to change
    setting.

\13
                          RECEIVE

    Can be set to any frequency starting at the lowest
    end of the RF range and increases in increments
    determined by MINIMUM CHANNEL SPACE (see COMMON
    DATA, HARDWARE menu).  If unable to set exact freq-
    uency, select smaller channel spacing.


\14
                         TRANSMIT

    Can be set to any frequency starting at the lowest
    end of the RF range and increases in increments
    determined by MINIMUM CHANNEL SPACE (see COMMON
    DATA, HARDWARE menu).  If unable to set exact freq-
    uency, select smaller channel spacing.


\15

                       POWER SETTING

    Each channel may be set to a different power setting.
    The current choices are 0W (TX inhibited), 0.5W,
    1.0W, 2.0W, 6.0W, 10W, 25W and 40W.  When the 'A' key
    is programmed for Low Power switching, it only takes
    effect if the low power level is less than the
    programmed channel power.

\16
                         CHANNEL TAG

    8 digit alpha tag displayed on radio front panel.
    Valid digits are:  A-Z, 0-9, and the following
    characters: " $ () * + , - / <> = ? ^.

\17
                       SUBAUDIO TYPE

    Use space bar or right mouse button to select
    CTCSS/DCS/NONE.

\18
                          DECODE

    Press space bar or right mouse button to change;
    select from menu window with <ды.

    If User Defined CTCSS is selected, pressing <ды 
    will bring up the User Defined CTCSS menu.  This
    menu is also accessible from the COMMON DATA, 
    MISCELLANEOUS menu.

\19
                          ENCODE

    Press space bar or right mouse button to change;
    select from menu window with <ды.

    If User Defined CTCSS is selected, pressing <ды
    will bring up the User Defined CTCSS menu.  This
    menu is also accessible from the COMMON DATA, 
    MISCELLANEOUS menu.

\20
			Transmit TimeOut Timer Flag

    Toggle ON (Ш) or OFF (-) with space bar or right
    mouse button.  If ON, time out timer is in effect
    for that channel.  Set the TOT timer in the COMMON
    DATA, MISCELLANEOUS  menu.

\21
                    PENALTY TIMER FLAG

    Toggle ON (Ш) or OFF (-) with space bar or right
    mouse button.  If ON and time out timer is exceeded
    this is the amount of time that must expire before
    PTT will key the Transmitter again.  Keying the radio
    before the release time is over will reset this
    timer, thus increasing the total time before PTT is
    active again. Set the penalty timer in the COMMON
    DATA, MISCELLANEOUS menu.

    Note that the PENALTY Timer has no effect if the TOT
    flag is not selected.

\22
                    RELEASE TIMER FLAG

    Toggle ON (Ш) or OFF (-) with space bar or right
    mouse button.  If ON, the amount of time that must
    expire after unkeying the radio before PTT becomes
    active again.  Keying the radio before the release
    time is over will reset the release timer thus
    increasing the total time before PTT is active again.
    Set the release timer in the COMMON DATA, MIS-
    CELLANEOUS menu.


\23
                         SCAN FLAG

    Toggle ON (Ш) or OFF (-) with space bar or right
    mouse button.  If ON, the channel will initially be
    selected for scan. If user scan edit is enabled
    (see COMMON DATA, SCANNING menu) the user may
    over-write these selections-they will only be the
    INITIAL settings. If user scan edit is disabled,
    these will be the only channels the user can scan.
    See GROUP menu, SCAN GROUP SELECTIONS for initial
    group scan selections.

\24
                   SEQUENTIAL TONE FLAG

    Toggle ON (Ш) or OFF (-) with space bar or right
    mouse button.  If ON, all of the decode ID's that
    are programmed with data will be in effect for THIS
    channel only. The following encode ID's will also
    be in effect for this channel:
                   On Hook ID
                   Off Hook ID
                   Acknowledge ID (Transpond)
                   Reset Ack ID (Auto Interrogate)

\25
                         BCLO/BTLO

    Use the space bar or right mouse button to select
    either Busy Channel Lock Out (BCLO), Busy Tone Lock
    Out (BTLO), or none.  BCLO will not allow the user
    to transmit in he presence of carrier.  BTLO will
    allow the user to transmit in the presence of
    carrier ONLY if proper tone is detected also
    (repeater hang time). Selecting NONE allows the
    user to transmit anytime.

\26
                       CHANNEL LOCK

    Use the space bar or right mouse button to select
    or deselect.  If lock is ON, that channel will NOT
    be accessible by the user although it will still be
    programmed.  Use this feature to program different
    radio access levels from one configuration.

    In Voting/Scanning schemes (European versions only),
    locked channels may be included in a Vote Group.  
    The locked flag will be ignored by the radio when 
    the voting channel is selected and so will select
    a locked channel if the strongest signal is present
    on that channel, and will operate as normal.
    However, the locked channels will NOT be accessible
    directly by using the front panel up/down keys, or
    keypad.

\27

                     GROUP ALPHA TAGS

    Enter the group alpha tags that will be displayed
    for two secondss whenever the current group is
    changed on the radio (group button is pressed).
    Valid  characters are:  A-Z, 0-9, and the following:
                " $ () * + , - / <> = ? ^.

\28

                    CHANNEL ALLOCATION

    Allocate the number of channels in each group.
    Group 1 MUST have at least 1 channel.  The other 98
    channels can be divided up in any configuration
    among the other 9 groups.  If channels are
    allocated to a group, you must program at least 1
    of those channels before downloading the
    information to the radio.

\29

                       SCAN MODE

    Allows dealer to set up initial groups to be
    scanned;  if user scan edit is disabled, you must
    set up the groups that the user will be allowed to
    scan during GROUP SCAN.  If user scan edit is
    enabled, these will be the initial settings, but
    the user will be able to alter them.

\30

                       SAVE TO DISK

    Use the <TAB> key or the mouse to move between the
    search specification, the current list of saved
    files meeting that specification, or to enter the
    name of the file you wish to save.  Only valid DOS
    file names will be allowed (no spaces in the name,
    and only characters A-Z, 0-9, the special char-
    acters: "-" and "_".

\31

                    RETRIEVE FROM DISK

    Use the <TAB> key or the mouse to move between the
    search specification, the current list of saved
    files meeting that specification, or to enter the
    name of the file you wish to load.  Only valid DOS
    file names will be allowed (no spaces in the name,
    and only characters A-Z, 0-9, the special char-
    acters: "-" and "_".

\32

                           PRINT

    Allows you to the current radio configuration to
    your printer.  Ready printer and select "OK".

\33

                         CONFIGURE

    Allows you to select different communication ports
    to send/receive radio data to/from and to select
    different printer ports.

    Allows you to select COLOUR of MONO type of display
    to best suit your computer system.

\34

    FREQ BAND: Select Low band, VHF, Band 3 (220 MHz),
    UHF, 800 or 900 MHz.  When changing frequency band,
    the next 4 parameters will automatically be set by
    the program to the correct settings.

\35

    REF OSC XTAL: Do not alter this setting after the
    frequency band selection has set the value unless
    you are doing a special modification to the radio.

\36

    FIRST IF FREQ: Do not alter this setting after the
    frequency band selection has set the value unless
    you are doing a special modification to the radio.
    Divide by numbers are not downloaded to the radio,
    but computed by the radio using this parameter and
    the channel information downloaded.  If set
    improperly, receiver will not function.

\37
    LO INJECTION: Do not alter this setting after the
    frequency band selection has set the value unless
    you are doing a special modification to the radio.
    Change this setting to eliminate image problems in
    some areas.  Radio will have to be retuned after
    modification to this setting, and some radios are
    not capable of altering.  This information is used
    in conjunction with FIRST IF, REF OSC FREQ and
    channel information to compute divide by numbers.

\38

    PRESCALER: Do not alter this setting after the
    frequency band selection has set the value unless
    you are doing a special modification to the radio.

\39
    CHANNEL SPACING:  Used to set the minimum channel
    step size.  When setting TX and RX frequencies,
    program calculates the distance from the LOWEST
    permitted RF frequency in these preset channel
    steps.  If unable to set the exact frequency, or
    when setting for "splits",  change the channel
    spacing to match the desired minimum step.

\40
    MINIMUM VOLUME: Allows the dealer to set a minimum
    volume level so the operator cannot turn the radio
    completely down.

\41
    BEEP VOLUME: Set the volume of the error tones, beeps
    and DTMF generation (if programmed) heard when press-
    ing the keys.  To disable the keypress beeps, see
    COMMON DATA, FRONT PANEL menu.

\42
    POWER CALIBRATION: Used to fine tune the low power
    settings for exact values.  

\43
    WRITE COUNT: Radio will keep track of the number of
    times the EEPROM is written to.  As EEPROM nears
    the marginal part of it's life (В10,000 writes) the
    front panel will alert the operator to contact the
    Vertex dealer for service to prevent misoperation.

\44
                     SCAN MODE

    Radios may be programmed to scan in two different
    ways: USA or Euro.  The scan methods are summarised
    as follows:

    EURO: This is a simple scan mode where the radio 
          simply steps through each channel in the Scan 
          List.  When it detects a valid signal (ie the 
          presence of carrier plus valid CTCSS/DCS tone, 
          if programmed), then the scanning will pause.

          The Scan List is programmed by the user and not
          by CE-7.

          When paused on a channel, the Dwell Time, Dual
          Watch enable/disable, Dual Watch frequency and 
          the Talkback channel can be specified.

    USA:  This mode has four sub-modes:

              . Sc : Scan all CE-7 selected channels.
              . Ur : Scan all user selected channels.
              . SP : Scan all CE-7 selected channels with
                     priority (CE-7 selected).
              . UP : Scan all user selected channels with
                     priority (user selected).

\45
                      DWELL PERIOD

    This is the period of time that the radio will
    remain paused on an active channel. After this
    period, regardless if there is activity or not, the
    scanning will resume. If the activity ceases before
    the dwell period expires, then the radio will remain
    for two seconds (or less if the dwell period expires
    first), before resuming the scan. 

    If this parameter is set to zero then the radio will
    remain paused on an active channel for as long as
    there is activity. When the activity stops the two
    second 'hang' time shall take effect as normal. 

\46
                       DUAL WATCH

    This enables or disables priority channel checking
    when the radio is paused on an active channel during 
    scanning.

\47
              PRIORITY CHANNEL CHECK FREQUENCY

    When paused on an active channel during scanning, the
    radio may be programmed to check the priority 
    channel(s).  This parameter sets how often the 
    priority channel(s) is (are) checked.

\48
                      SCAN SPEED

    This sets the period that the radio will stay on a
    channel to check for activity (ie the presence of
    carrier). If your radio is 'missing' active channels
    try lengthening this parameter. 

    Note that the radio automatically lengthens this
    parameter when checking channels that it is
    programmed to decode CTCSS or DCS. It will only
    pause on an active channel that has the appropriate
    CTCSS/DCS tone/code. 

\49
                        TALKBACK

    This determines which channel the radio will switch
    to when the user attempts to transmit while the
    radio is paused on an active channel during scanning.

    Home Channel:    This is the channel that was
                     selected before scanning was
                     started.

    Priority:        The radio will transmit on the
                     Priority 1 channel.  If no channel
                     has been assigned level 1 priority,
                     the radio will transmit on the
                     Priority 2 channel.  If no priority
                     channels have been assigned then the
                     radio will transmit on the Home
                     Channel.

    Stopped Channel  The radio will transmit on the
                     channel that it is paused on.


\50
    
	SPARE - If you can read this then the program 
     has crashed !

\51

	SPARE - If you can read this then the program 
     has crashed !


\52
    DWELL or RESUME TIMER:  When scanning and radio is
    on hook, the amount of time the radio will wait for
    another transmission before resuming scan operation.

\53
    SCAN SPEED: The amount of time that the radio stays
    on a scan channel to check for the presence of a 
    valid signal.  Some radios with widely spaced channels
    may require slower SCAN SPEEDS (ie spend longer on
    each channel).  50mS is a good value to start with.

\54
    PRIORITY TIMER: If a priority is selected and the
    radio stops scanning on a non-priority channel, it
    will check the priority channels for activity at
    this interval.  Press <RETURN> to change setting.

\55
    OFF HOOK REVERT: The channel to revert to when the
    microphone is taken off-hook.  Selecting HOME
    CHANNEL will revert to the last group/channel
    selected before starting scan.  LAST BUSY will
    revert to the group/channel where activity was
    last detected; if no channel was received since
    power on, radio will default to HOME CHANNEL.
    Selecting PRIORITY will revert to P1 in the current
    group; if no P1 is selected, radio will revert to
    P2.  If no P2 is selected, it will revert to HOME
    CHANNEL.

\56
    REVERT IMMEDIATE:

    If this flag is set then the unit will revert to the
    programmed revert channel (Home / Priority / Last 
    Busy) regardless or whether there is carrier present
    or not.  If the flag is not set, then the radio will
    only revert to the programmed revert channel if there
    is NO carrier, otherwise it will stay on the busy
    channel.

\57
    PRIORITY REVERT TIMER: If off-hook revert is set to
    HOME or LAST BUSY, the amount of non-activity (TX
    or RX) before the radio will revert to a priority
    channel (if one is programmed).  Receiver activity
    PTT or On-hook resets the timer.

\58
    USER SCAN PROG: Enables\disables the ability of the
    user to modify the scan list (either group or
    channel).  If disabled, you must preselect which
    channels are to be marked for scan (see main
    channel menu).  This will also apply to group scan
    selection; if USER PROG is disabled, you must
    select which groups will be selected for scan.  If
    scan prog is enabled, you may still preset which
    channels will be scanned, but the user may alter
    the scan list by pressing the SCAN button while
    turning the radio on.

\59
    USER PRI PROG: Enable\disables the ability of the
    user to modify the priorities.  This function is
    independent of the scan list programming.  If
    disabled, you must select which channels will be
    priorities (up to 2 may be set PER GROUP).  If
    enabled, you may still preset the priorities, but
    the user may alter them through the front panel.

\60
                     VOTE DWELL PERIOD

    This is the time between searching and voting on the 
    strongest signal.

\61
	                   VOTE GROUPS

    There may be up to ten vote groups programmed each of
    ten channels.  Channels programmed as voting channels
    cannot be included as part of a vote group.

\62
                  TRANSMIT OPTION WHEN VOTING

    Setting this flag allows you to transmit even when no
    carrier is present on the system.  This is included
    for systems that require the mobiles to initiate
    calls.

\63
	                    VOTING TYPE

    There are two types of Voting Search & Lock systems:

    Carrier:    The radio will search the (up to) ten 
                channels in the vote group and select the
                channel with the strongest signal.

    Free Tone:  The radio will search the (up to) ten 
                channels in the vote group but only vote 
                on those channels that have the Free Tone
                frequency.


\64
                          FREE TONE

    This is the frequency of the tone which is
    transmitted by the base station to indicate that it 
    is free.  This parameter is only used on Free Tone 
    voting systems.

\65
                     INTER FREE TONE GAP

    This parameter specifies how long the radio will 
    tolerate the absence of the Free Tone on the voting 
    system.  If the Free Tone disappears for greater than
    this time, then the channel is deemed to be busy (in 
    use) and the radio will attempt to find another
    channel that is free.

\66

    TONE SYSTEM: Selects table of standard tones in
    user in the Land Mobile Industry.  To define custom
    tone table, select "User".  Tones initially
    displayed in User table are tone for last system
    selected.  Table will have 15 tones + none.  When
    programming the encode identities, select the
    number of the tone listed in the table.

\67

    TONE TIMERS: Used for ENCODE identities only.  This
    table of 8 tone durations is used along with tone
    system table to create encode identity tones and
    durations.

\68
                     DECODE IDENTITIES

    NOTE: All of the decode ID's are selectable on a
    per channel basis EXCEPT for KILL and RESURRECT
    which are radio wide and independent of the SQ flag
    set in the main channel menu.  Other decode ID's
    may be disabled by programming all tone numbers
    blank (use the <DEL> key). These functions
    (including KILL and RESURRECT) will be disabled for
    all channels.

    INDIVIDUAL CALL: Selective call ID.  Up to 17
    sequential tones can be enabled; enter tone # from
    tone system table or use <DEL> key to erase unwant-
    ed tones.  On CHANNEL DATA menu, selecting SQ (Ш)
    will enable all decode identities for that channel.
    Individual call can be acknowledged (see encode
    ID's and TRANSPOND).

    GROUP CALL: Same as INDIVIDUAL CALL ID except this
    ID will activate for a group call ID from the dis-
    patcher. Group calls can not be ACKed.

    REMOTE RESET: Decode sequence that will reset radio
    after individual or group call ID has been decoded.
    Remote reset can be acknowledged (see encode ID's
    and AUTO INTERROGATE).

    PAGE TRIGGER: Decode sequence that will cause radio
    to go to paging channel (see Main Menu/Sequential
    Tones) and send the paging Encode ID (see ENCODE
    ID's).

    RESURRECT: Decode sequence to bring radio back into
    operation after decoding a "KILL" sequence.

    KILL: Decode sequence that will cause radio to
    become totally inoperable until resurrected or re-
    programmed.  A.K.A. deadbeat disable. Radio will
    display "KILLED" until reprogrammed or RESURRECTED
    and will alert the user with a sequence of appro-
    priate tones (with apologies to Chopin).

\69
                     ENCODE IDENTITIES

    NOTE: To disable any of these features, delete all
    of the tone numbers from that item.  The function
    will be disabled for ALL channels normally enabled
    for sequential tone. See each ID for per channel
    operation or radio wide operation.


    OFF HOOK: If "A" button is programmed for CALL/
    RESET, pressing the "A" button while off hook will
    send this tone sequence.  See COMMON DATA/FRONT
    PANEL Menu for "A" button programming. This feature
    is set on a per channel basis by the SQ flag (see
    main channel menu).

    ON HOOK: Same as OFF-HOOK except the sequence will
    be sent when ON-HOOK.

    ACKNOWLEDGE: The encode sequence that is sent in
    response to decoding an INDIVIDUAL CALL ID.  This
    will only be sent if TRANSPOND is set to YES (see
    Main Menu/Sequential tone). This feature is set on
    a per channel basis by the SQ flag, main channel
    menu.

    RESET ACK: The encode sequence sent in response to
    decoding a remote RESET ID (see Decode ID's).  This
    will only be sent if AUTO INTERROGATE is set to YES
    (see Main Menu/Sequential tone). This feature is
    selectable on a per channel basis.

    PAGE ID: The tone sequence sent in response to
    decoding a PAGE TRIGGER ID (see Decode ID's). It
    will be sent on the paging channel selected in the
    sequential tone main menu. This feature is
    dependent on the setting of the PAGE TRIGGER only
    and is radio wide.

    PTT ID: The tone sequence sent if PTT ANI or
    RELEASE ANI is set to ON (see sequential tone main
    menu).  This feature is dependent on the settings
    of the ANI selections only and are independent of
    the SQ flag (they will be radio wide).

    ALARM ID: Encode sequence sent if "A" button is
    programmed for ALARM and "A" button is pressed
    twice within 1/2 second.  See COMMON DATA/FRONT
    PANEL menu for A button programming.  This feature
    is radio wide and dependent of the "A" button
    programming only.

    LOG ON ID: Tone sequence sent when radio is first
    turned on. This feature is selectable on a per
    channel basis and is affected by the SQ flag and
    INITIAL CHANNEL setting (see Miscellaneous menu).

\70
                      STATUS PARAMETERS

    This menu allows you to specify the initial display 
    mode, initial status value and reversion time.


\71

                   CHANNEL ACCESS MODE

    This entry allows you to select or deselect the 
    Channel Access Mode.  Select this allows you
    to enter the CHANNEL ACCESS MENU.

\72
                      CHANNEL ACCESS

    NOTE: All channel access functions are in effect
    for only those channels marked for sequential tone
    in the Main Menu (SQ:Ш).  Changing these settings
    will determine how the radio acts during selective
    call, when the mic is on/off hook, and restrict the
    user from transmitting or hearing other
    conversations.

\73
                   INDIVIDUAL CALL ALERT

    Determines how the radio will alert the user during
    a selective call sequence.

\74
                     GROUP CALL ALERT

    Determines if the radio will alert the user after a
    group call sequence is decoded.

\75
                      PAGING CHANNEL
    This is the channel that the radio will revert to
    and send the PAGE ID (if programmed) after receipt
    of the PAGE TRIGGER ID (if programmed).  See DECODE
    and ENCODE ID's respectively.

\76
                        ANI ON PTT

    Determines whether PTT encode ID is sent every time
    PTT is pressed.

\77
                      ANI ON RELEASE

    Determines whether PTT ID is sent every time PTT is
    released {SEND} or a single short burst tone is
    sent on release {PIP ONLY}, or no ANI is sent on
    release {NONE}.

\78
                         PTT DELAY
    The amount of delay after the radio is keyed before
    sending any encode ID's.  Range is 0-1700mS.

\79
                         TRANSPOND

    Determines if the ACKNOWLEDGE ID is sent in
    response to an INDIVIDUAL CALL ID being decoded.

\80
                     AUTO INTERROGATE

    Determines if the RESET ACK ID is sent in response
    to a REMOTE RESET ID being decoded.

\81
    A Key programming: Determines operation of the "A"
    button; can be set for ALARM, CALL/RESET (sequent-
    ial tone), LOW POWER, CTCSS ENCODE DISABLE/ENABLE,
    CONTINUE SCAN, and TALK AROUND for semi-duplex
    channels.  If set for ALARM several parameters will
    also need to be programmed (see help function,
    Alarm programming).

\82
    EMERGENCY ENABLE/DISABLE:  If enabled the user may
    press the EMERG (European version) key, or the 
    A key twice within 1/2 sec (USA version), to initiate
    the Emergency Mode.

\83
    HOOK SWITCH ENABLE/DISABLE: If disabled, will
    always be "on hook" and will ignore any off-hook
    transitions.

\84
    ON/OFF disable: Disables the radio from being
    switched off via the front panel volume control.

\85
    KEY BEEP: Enable/disable the "beep" whenever a key
    is pressed.  Set volume of the beep in the COMMON
    DATA, HARDWARE menu.

\86
    ALLOW USER PROGRAMMING OF A KEY:  This flag enables
    or disables the user from being allowed to re-
    programme the A key from the Special Functions menu
    from the Front Panel

\87
    COUNTRY CODE:  Only for dealer use; stored in
    EEPROM, it is used for identifying country of
    origin or use.

\88
    MARKET APPLICATION: Only for dealer use; stored in
    EEPROM can be used as an additional aid for ident-
    ifying how radio is grouped or used.

\89
    ID SCHEDULE: If not set for 0, will be displayed on
    power up for display revert time to indicate prog-
    ramming configuration.  For identification purposes
    only.

\90
    SERIAL NUMBER: For Yaesu use only.

\91
    DEFAULT SPLIT: When setting RX frequency, this
    offset will automatically be applied to the trans-
    mit frequency.  Can be positive or negative.

\92
    USER CTCSS: Program the 3 custom CTCSS frequencies.
    Range is 67.0 to 260 Hz in .1 Hz increments.

\93
    CTCSS SQUELSH TAIL ELIMINTATION.  When this flag is
    set, the radio will transmit approximately 150mS of
    pure carrier, ie no CTCSS.  This will cause all 
    receiving units to mute their speaker (because the
    CTCSS is no longer present) before the squelch mutes
    the unit.  It eliminates the noisy 'squelch tail'
    which is sometimes present at the end of each over.

\94
    AUTO SWITCH OFF: Used to automatically switch the
    radio OFF if no user activity is detected after 8
    hours to prevent battery drain from occurring.

\95
    BUSY ALERT: Determines whether alert tone is heard
    when user keys the radio and transmitter is inhib-
    ited via BCLO or BTLO.

\96
    EXTERNAL PTT: Used to control PTT routing through
    External accessory connector.  Accessory connector
    has PTT out and PTT in.  If set for MIC & OPTION,
    radio will only transmit when mic is keyed AND PTT
    path is established through external connector.  If
    set for OPTION only, will transmit any time option
    PTT in is low.

\97
    TOT, RELEASE, and PENALTY: Timers used by the front
    panel flags to unkey the transmitter after PTT has
    been held for too long, the amount of time after
    PTT has been released before it can be keyed again,
    and the amount of time after TOT has unkeyed the
    radio before the user can transmit again.

    During release and penalty times, keying the radio
    before the time has expired resets the timer, thus
    increasing the total time needed before user can
    transmit again.

    TIME OUT TIMER: If set and enabled at the main
    channel menu, will unkey the radio after timer has
    expired.  If TOT expires, the user may be inhibited
    from rekeying right away (see PENALTY TIMER).  The
    user may also be inhibited from rekeying after
    release of PTT normally (with out TOT expiration)
    by setting the RELEASE timer and enabling it on the
    main channel menu.  TOT is selectable on a per
    channel basis.

\98
    PENALTY TIMER: If TOT causes the radio to unkey,
    the amount of time that expires before the user may
    transmit again.  Pressing PTT before PENALTY time
    is over causes the timer to RESET, thereby
    increasing the total penalty time. This feature is
    selectable on a per channel basis on the main
    channel menu.

\99
    RELEASE TIMER: If the user unkeys (to prevent TOT
    from expiring for instance) the amount of time
    required before they may key the radio again.
    Pressing PTT before RELEASE time is over causes the
    timer to RESET thereby increasing the total release
    time.  This feature is selectable on a per channel
    basis on the main channel menu.

\100
    SWITCH ON CHANNEL:  The channel to revert to every-
    time the radio is switched on. 0=Last channel.

\101
    SIGNALLING TYPE: Used to select either sequential
    tone or DTMF tones for the ENCODE ID's only.
    DECODE ID's will always be sequential type.

\102
    ALARM PARAMETERS:  Set the number of Alarm cycles,
    transmit time, receive time and Alarm frequency.

\103
    LIVE TX TIME: Amount of time that TX and mic
    will remain active after alarm ID is sent.

\104
    RX LIVE TIME: Amount of time that RX is active
    before repeating alarm sequence.  The combin-
    ation of these 3 times determines the duty
    cycle of the alarm sequence.

\105
    RX DEAD TIME: Amount of time that RX is muted
    after transmission of alarm sequence; allows
    dispatcher to alert other vehicles without the
    initiating mobile hearing the response.

\106
    REPEAT COUNT: Number of times to repeat the
    above 3 sequences.

\107
    ALARM CHANNEL: Channel to send the alarm seq-
    uence on. 0=Home channel.

\108
    SWITCH OFF: Set to Inhibit to prevent radio
    in an alarm condition from being reset.  If
    switched off, front panel will blank, but
    radio will continue to send alarm sequence.

\109
    LOW BAND: Frequency range is 29.7 to 50 Mhz. The
    following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         16.9 MHz
                 LO INJ:         HIGH SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      64/65
                 MIN CH STEP:    20 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  Simplex

\110
    EURO LO BAND (MID BAND): Frequency range is 66 to
    88 MHz.  The following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         16.9 MHz
                 LO INJ:         HIGH SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      64/65
                 MIN CH STEP:    20 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  Simplex

\111
    VHF: Frequency range is 134 to 174 MHz. The
    following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         21.4 MHz
                 LO INJ:         LOW SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      64/65
                 MIN CH STEP:    5 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  Simplex

\112
    BAND III (EUROPE): Frequency range is 174 to 250
    MHz. The following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         21.9 MHz
                 LO INJ:         LOW SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      64/65
                 MIN CH STEP:    5 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  Simplex

\113
    UHF: Frequency range is 400 to 512 MHz. The
    following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         47.9 MHz
                 LO INJ:         LOW SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      64/65
                 MIN CH STEP:    12.5 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  5 MHz

\114
    800 MHz: Frequency range is 806 to 870 MHz. The
    following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         45.1 MHz
                 LO INJ:         LOW SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      128/129
                 MIN CH STEP:    12.5 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  -45 MHz

\115
    900 MHz: Frequency range is 889 to 947 MHz. The
    following will be set automatically:

                 REF OSC:        12.8 MHz
                 1st IF:         39.1 MHz
                 LO INJ:         LOW SIDE
                 PRESCALER:      128/129
                 MIN CH STEP:    12.5 kHz
                 DEFAULT SPLIT:  -39 MHz

\116
                   STATUS ON SWITCH-OFF

    The status value may be discarded at switch-off (so 
    that the status number will default to zero at 
    switch-on), or saved (so that the value will be 
    remembered at switch-on).

\117
                   INITIAL DISPLAY MODE

    The display may be force to display status, or 
    channel number at switch-on.  This parameter 
    specifies which one.

\118
           CHANNEL -> STATUS DISPLAY REVERSION

    This value specifies the length of time that the 
    channel number will be displayed for before the 
    display reverts to displaying the Status number.

    Pressing any key resets this timer.

    Setting this value to zero disables reversion.

\119
                      STATUS MESSAGES

    Up to 30 messages may be programmed,  which will be 
    displayed on the radio when it decodes a valid status
    code message.

\120
    INITIAL CONDITION: Determines if the receiver is
    muted when radio is turned on.

\121
    RESET TIMER: Amount of time before receiver will
    automatically mute after receiving a selective
    call.  0= disabled.

\122
    SWITCH ON RESET: If reset timer is not zero,
    enabling this function will cause radio to mute
    after reset time expires.

\123
    OPEN ON CALL: If set to OPEN, will cause receiver
    to unmute when "A" button is pressed (A button must
    be programmed for CALL/RESET).

\124
    OPEN ON PTT: If set to OPEN, will cause receiver to
    unmute when PTT is released.

\125
    OPEN OFF HOOK: If set to OPEN, will cause receiver
    to unmute when mic goes off-hook.

\126
    CLOSE ON HOOK: If set to CLOSE, will cause receiver
    to mute when mic goes on-hook.

\127
    CALL LAMP ON RESET: If set to EXTINGUISH, will
    cause the "CALL" icon to stop flashing after
    receiving a REMOTE RESET decode ID (receiver will
    always mute).

\128
    3RD TONE RESET: If enabled, will cause all radios
    that correctly decode first 3 tones in a 5/6 tone
    sequence to mute their receivers.  This will ensure
    privacy in a selective call system by setting the
    1st 3 tones of a radio group the same, and only the
    last 2/3 digits determining the individual call ID
    will open the mute after each paging sequence.
